{
  "_why": "Per-source scores against the compat corpus, asserted by crates/dfe-transforms/tests/compat_corpus.rs. It is a RATCHET: a source may only improve. The scores are only meaningful against the pipelines and engine named below, so the assertion is skipped when the corpus on disk was captured against anything else. Raise the numbers when they improve; lowering one needs a stated reason, the way a fixture baseline does.",
  "_lowered": "Six sources were lowered when compare-policy.yaml gained per-source scoping. event.duration was excluded corpus-wide for an azure overflow, and event.kind and error.message for a cisco_nexus date failure, so 4,720 fields were never compared on any source. Scoping those three entries to the sources their reasons name restored them and made 54 events visibly wrong that had been passing on the strength of the blindness -- zscaler_zia 92 to 68, aws 515 to 494, m365_defender 100 to 96, microsoft_defender_endpoint 20 to 17, checkpoint 107 to 105. The transforms did not get worse; the measurement got true. TWO more on 2026-09-06, both set by hand because the tool refuses a fall and both are the gate working rather than a regression. servicenow 31/170 to 32/172: the first whole-tree regeneration brought its three dead guards to life, so processors that had never run now run and their output is compared -- it gains an EVENT and two wrong fields with it, which is what a newly-live guard does. never_ran 474 to 477 against a CHANGED POPULATION: the same regeneration resolved every script the generator recognises to its runner at generation time, taking ladder call sites from 3,490 to 2,475, so the set this counts is no longer the set it counted. There is no --resize for never_ran the way there is for a source whose event total moved. | never_ran RAISED 425 to 426 with the gitlab date-abort fix, which is the gate working rather than a regression: gitlab.production aborted on its date processor, so two scripts BEHIND it had never been reached. The same run shows distinct scripts 1197 to 1199, so the population grew by two and one of those two binds a pattern it does not apply. raise_baseline.py refuses a rise, correctly, so this one is recorded here by hand with its reason. | never_ran RAISED 413 to 415 when literal_value was anchored to the WHOLE right-hand side, and this one is the gate working with the population UNCHANGED at 1,200 distinct scripts -- two scripts that ran now do not. literal_value is the last reader parse_rhs tries, so an expression none of the others could read arrived there intact and came back as the first quoted run inside it: every candidate in the never-ran set would have written a fragment of its own expression as the field's value -- event.severity as the literal 'Unknown', event.action as ':', host.os.platform as ' ', file.extension as '.', client.mac as '-'. No source regressed on the same run and digital_guardian gained three fields, so the two that stopped running were writing a wrong value and now write none. The number falls again as those scripts get matchers of their own. | never_ran RAISED 412 to 413 with the whole-tree regeneration that brought the flat-subscript transpiler into the tree, against a CHANGED POPULATION -- distinct ladder scripts 1,200 to 1,188, because the generator now resolves twelve more of them to their runners at generation time. The set this counts is no longer the set it counted, which is the same reading as the 474-to-477 entry above. The regeneration itself lost nothing: ti_eclecticiq 6/33 to 33/33 events, sysdig +63 fields, elastic_agent +1 event, and no source moved down. | FIVE sources lowered and never_ran raised 366 to 395 when the CAPTURE ENVELOPE was fixed, which moves the corpus itself rather than any transform. scripts/compat.py guessed 'already a Beats document' from a single `message` key; upstream's elastic-package wraps EVERY entry of a .log fixture into `message` without inspecting it (internal/testrunner/runners/pipeline/testcase.go, readTestCaseEntriesForRawInput) and dispatches on file extension alone. The rule now is `@metadata`, or `message` as the ONLY key, or a .json fixture. A second defect went with it: config_for discarded the shared test-common-config.yml whenever a fixture had its own, where elastic-package unpacks both into one struct (testconfig.go) and go-ucfg merges maps by key and lists BY POSITION -- that is what made microsoft_dhcp's timezone look missing. The headline FALLS, 24052/25032 events to 24072/25225, and that is the point: 60 upstream-verbatim payloads across 29 packages had been passed through un-wrapped, so Elasticsearch built event.original out of the prose line inside and threw Jackson `Unrecognized token`. Captures Elasticsearch errored on outright go 119 to 45, fields CORRECT rise 657,114 to 684,314, and five sources score for the first time because their whole capture had been an error -- backstage 20/21, ece 3/39, elastic_security 1/1, microsoft_dhcp 32/32, nextron_thor 22/30. Twelve more were resized, and ONE of those twelve hides a fall that wants stating, because --resize carries no ratchet check: symantec_endpoint_security goes 52/58 events to 3/60 while its fields CORRECT rise 23,056 to 26,342 of 26,658. Only two of those events are new; the other 58 went from 52 matching to 3, so nearly every event is now wrong in a field or two rather than whole. Its seven category fixtures had each been scoring 100% against a capture Elasticsearch built from an un-wrapped payload. It is the largest piece of debt this change makes visible and it is the same mechanism as the three INPUT cases below. The five lowered here fall at a CONSTANT denominator, for two different reasons. The EXPECTATION was wrong for two: atlassian_cloud 10/10 to 0/10 and citrix_adc 189/189 to 187/189, whose old captures were the vendor payload echoed back carrying the pipeline's own failure -- atlassian_cloud held `error.message` reading 'Processor json with tag parse_event_original_json failed', and citrix_adc's system stream held `system.auxtemp0` where it should hold `citrix_adc.system.cpu.utilization.pct`. Scored against upstream's own committed -expected.json, which knows nothing of our transforms, the old captures agreed on 8-14% of compared paths for atlassian_cloud and 2.4% for citrix_adc.system, and the new ones on 100%. The INPUT was unrealistic for the other three: kibana 7/7 to 0/7, platform_observability 3/3 to 0/3, envoyproxy 7/7 to 5/7, whose expectations are unchanged bar event.ingested, which is excluded corpus-wide, and which agree with upstream 100% both before and after. What moved is what the transform is FED -- the payload now arrives as a string in `message` the way a Beats delivery sends it, instead of pre-decoded at the top level. Those three passed only because nothing had ever asked them to decode it, so this is debt becoming visible rather than debt being created. never_ran 366 to 395 against a GROWN population, 1,192 distinct scripts to 1,246: the newly-scoring and resized sources put 54 more scripts in front of the runtime and 29 of them bind a pattern they never apply, which is the gate working. raise_baseline.py refuses a fall and refuses a never_ran rise, correctly, so those six numbers are set here by hand. | never_ran RAISED 344 to 345 when the event started answering `_ingest.timestamp`, and the population grew by exactly one script with it, 1,246 to 1,247. Elasticsearch stamps that field before the first processor runs and nothing upstream of a transform could, so every `set` reading its template wrote the empty string -- which cost ti_misp 13 events, because the date processor behind it failed and appended `error.message` while `misp.attribute.decayed` was never written at all. The one added script is extrahop_investigation's, reading `ctx.temp.ingest_time`, whose guard could never open: that field is set from the same template and the empty value was skipped, so the script had never been REACHED and could not be in the set this counts. The dump reads `ran: 0, skipped: 4` for it and extrahop holds at 3/7 with 4 fields wrong, so it declines all four and writes nothing -- the gate working. Catalogue diff over the same corpus: handled UNCHANGED at 46,204, no script stopped running, no script started, exactly one appeared. TOTAL 24,390 to 24,403 events and 2,191 to 2,176 extras, all of it ti_misp, which goes 44/59 to 57/59. The corpus harness PINS the minted instant (`PINNED_INGEST_INSTANT` in compat_corpus.rs) so the comparison does not drift with the calendar, while production stays on the clock the way Elasticsearch does. | never_ran RAISED 334 to 335 when GroupRecords bound ti_opencti's observable fold, and it is the gate working with the population GROWN. That one script builds `opencti.observable.<entity>` from a runtime value, and lines 430 to 2340 of the module -- the whole back half of the transform -- are gated on `has_value` over it, so nothing behind it had ever run. Binding it took ti_opencti 10/31 to 23/31 and its wrong fields 138 to 15, and the three field families a previous reading called `not Painless` (related.hosts, related.ip and the threat.indicator.url.* chain) went to 100% with nobody touching them. The catalogue grew 1,248 distinct scripts to 1,251 as those sub-pipelines began executing, handled ROSE 46,263 to 46,295, and of the three newly-reachable scripts two bind a pattern they never apply -- net +1. raise_baseline.py refuses a rise, correctly, so it is set here by hand. | never_ran RAISED 281 to 284 when parse_single_copy was gated on a BARE-PATH value, with the population UNCHANGED at 1,257 distinct scripts. Nothing the corpus measures moved: all 2,508 per-source and per-fixture score lines are identical, and TOTAL holds at 24,602/25,225 events and 687,761/693,657 fields with 1,431 extras and 0 errors. What moved is the accounting -- handled 46,366 to 46,092, skipped 7,470 to 7,744, which is 274 claims that wrote nothing. try_guarded_copy answered true for every event whose guard did not open, because the fallback READ the script and the runner reported the read rather than the write. Seven scripts shed those phantom runs and three fall to zero. Two of the three carry their source field nowhere in the corpus and so never legitimately ran: fortinet's advpnsc boolean over 92 events, and swimlane_audit_logs' lowercase sourceIp over 2, whose capitalised twin is the one holding data. The third was writing a WRONG value: painless_path answers the LAST ctx path in an expression, so jamf_compliance_reporter's epoch scale bound as a copy of json.header.time_milliseconds_offset and wrote 281 where 1,570,154,219,281 belonged, never running the multiply at all, on all 36 audit events. The corpus cannot see that one in either direction -- @timestamp is nondeterministic here and json.time_milliseconds is pruned before output -- so the number below is the only place the repair shows. raise_baseline.py refuses a rise, correctly, so it is set here by hand.",
